<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: choose one path</h2>
  <p class="sub">Every path ends with the three things listed under Show.</p>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">Answer the real argument</div>
    <p><a class="key-term" href="/foundations/eal/u5/#strawmanning" data-term="strawmanning" aria-expanded="false"><strong>Strawmanning</strong></a> means answering a weaker version of their argument instead of their real one.</p>
    <p><strong>Strawman:</strong> "Karnataka farmers just want to take all the water."<br><strong>Their real argument:</strong> "In a dry monsoon year our own reservoirs run low, so a fixed release downstream hurts us first."</p>
    <p><strong>Frames you can use:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>The strongest argument against ___ is ___, because ___.</li>
      <li>They are right that ___.</li>
      <li>I still disagree because ___, not because ___.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="choice-board">
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">A</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Counter-argument + Stakeholder Voice rehearsal (default)</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">Identify the strongest counter to your stakeholder's position. Write a paragraph stating it fairly, using the language and evidence that opposing stakeholder would use. Then rehearse with your class's Stakeholder Voice. Write your 2-sentence response without strawmanning.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Solo · Default</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">B</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Counter-argument with OPVL deepening (choice)</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">Same as Path A, plus OPVL the strongest counter-source (a piece of evidence the opposing stakeholder cites). The <a href="/ai-partners/the-source-evaluator/">Source Evaluator</a> walks the four letters. Specificity on the counter-source's Limitation strengthens your response paragraph significantly.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Solo · Choice</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice">
      <div class="choice-letter">C</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Stakeholder mapping peer-help (practice)</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">If the 6-field framework is fuzzy, come to the peer-help table. Fill in your first map row together with a peer + Ms. Jayanthi/Mr. Matt. The Murray-Darling worked example brief is the reference; it shows what specific (not generic) stakeholder analysis looks like.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Pairs · Practice</span>
    </div>
    <div class="choice" data-platform="boodlebox">
      <div class="choice-letter">D</div>
      <div class="choice-title">Boodle Build progress (stretch)</div>
      <div class="choice-detail">If you got an early start on your Boodle, push it further today: run the 5 test questions from the assignment, iterate the system prompt at least twice, document each iteration cycle. Aim to bring a Boodle that <em>sounds like</em> your stakeholder (not generic) to Block 8's peer test.</div>
      <span class="udl-tag">Solo · Stretch</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  {% include "partials/notebook-cue.njk" %}
</section>

<section class="block-section show">
  <span class="section-tag">Show</span>
  <h2>What you're submitting today</h2>
  <p class="sub">Hand in all three together.</p>
  <div class="submission-callout">
    <div class="label">Stakeholder map row + counter-argument + response</div>
    <p>(1) One fully-filled stakeholder-map row for your chosen stakeholder, all 6 fields: Who They Are · What They Want · Evidence They Cite · Position · Power · Allies+Opponents. (2) One counter-argument paragraph stating the strongest opposing view <em>fairly</em>, using the opposing stakeholder's framing and evidence (not yours). (3) A 2-sentence response to that counter <em>without strawmanning</em>: acknowledge what's legitimate, then articulate where you disagree on substance (not on the opposing stakeholder's competence or motives).</p>
    {% set submit = { id: "u5-b7", text: "Submit via Google Classroom" } %}{% include "partials/submit-link.njk" %}
  </div>
  <p class="rubric-meta">
    <strong>Rubric link:</strong> Today's counter-argument work is the T/T move the <a href="/units/unit-5/assessment-water-stakeholders/">Position Paper's "Acknowledge competing concerns" section</a> needs. T/T 7-8 requires "competing concerns acknowledged honestly." Strawmanning sits T/T in the middle bands. See the <a href="/foundations/the-rubric/">rubric</a>.
  </p>
</section>

